<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  arena: a 6 x 6 m walled arena with eight warehouse docks around the perimeter.

  Run it with:
    ros2 launch einride_mini_truck_bringup simulation.launch.py world:=arena

  Nothing else has to change - simulation.launch.py already takes the world
  basename as an argument, and no bridged topic is scoped to the world name.

  DOCK LAYOUT

  Two docks per wall, at +-1.5 m from each wall's midpoint. That is what makes
  the spacing genuinely even rather than merely symmetric: walking the 24 m
  perimeter, every consecutive pair is exactly 3.000 m apart, including across
  the corners (1.5 m to the corner, then 1.5 m on).

    letter  tag36h11 id  wall            pose (x, y, yaw)
    A       0            +X, south half   2.992, -1.500, pi
    B       1            +X, north half   2.992,  1.500, pi
    C       2            +Y, east half     1.500,  2.992, -pi/2
    D       3            +Y, west half    -1.500,  2.992, -pi/2
    E       4            -X, north half   -2.992,  1.500, 0
    F       5            -X, south half   -2.992, -1.500, 0
    G       6            -Y, west half    -1.500, -2.992, pi/2
    H       7            -Y, east half     1.500, -2.992, pi/2

  Each dock's +X is its approach direction, so the yaw values above are what turn
  each bay to face into the arena. The 8 mm inset comes from the dock frame
  sitting on the back wall's INNER face: the outer face is at dock x = -8 mm, so
  an origin 8 mm inside the arena wall puts the dock's back flush against it.

  All eight docks are geometrically identical - the trapezoidal bay is what
  distinguishes a dock from any other arena feature, not one dock from another.
  Telling them apart is the tag's job, and the pylon letter is the same
  information for a human. A scan matcher looking only at wall geometry can and
  will confuse dock A with dock E; that is a property of the arena to design
  around, not a bug in it.

  The models are generated - see einride_mini_truck_gazebo/CMakeLists.txt and
  dock_template/. DOCK_PLAN.md is the spec they are built to.

  PROP LAYOUT

  The dock ring above is exactly D4-symmetric - 4-fold rotation plus both mirrors,
  eight group elements - so a single 2D scan of it has up to eight equally-good pose
  solutions, and the arena is visually near-blank between the docks. The props below
  exist to break that and to give both sensors something to hold on to.

  Every corner carries a DIFFERENT arrangement, which is what breaks 4-fold rotation.
  NE != SW and NW != SE is what breaks the 180 deg element specifically - the one that
  props placed in mirror-symmetric pairs would leave intact. The NE triangle is also
  asymmetric about the y=x diagonal, which is the element that fixes NE in place.

    cluster  centre         contents                        footprint
    NE       ( 2.45,  2.45) 3 barrels in a triangle         x[2.16,2.74] y[2.21,2.74]
    NW       (-2.55,  2.55) 1 barrier, chamfering at 45 deg x[-2.86,-2.24] y[2.24,2.86]
    SW       (-2.45, -2.55) 2 barrels + 1 cone, in a line   x[-2.79,-2.14] y[-2.86,-2.21]
    SE       ( 2.45, -2.45) 1 barrel + 2 cones              x[2.09,2.81] y[-2.81,-2.14]
    interior (-0.85,  0.65) 2 barriers in an L + 1 barrel   x[-1.25,-0.46] y[0.28,1.12]

  The corners were free real estate: they carry no docks, and the nearest dock edge is
  1.1 m away. The interior cluster is the part that makes this a SLAM problem rather
  than a single-scan solve - it occludes, so the map is not observable from any one
  spot, and it forces a drive-around, which gives loop closure something to close.

  Gaps WITHIN a cluster are deliberately narrower than the rover (94-174 mm), so each
  cluster reads as one compound obstacle instead of a maze. The narrowest gap between
  different clusters is 1515 mm. Nothing intrudes on a dock's bay or on the 1.5 m x
  730 mm approach corridor in front of it - that was measured when these poses were
  chosen, and it is the thing to re-check by hand if any prop moves.

  Scales, and the measurements behind them, are in props/props.cmake and
  props/<dir>/AABB.md. All three props span the whole mandated 80-180 mm scan band and
  stay below the 300 mm arena wall. The cone is a weak LiDAR landmark on purpose -
  59 mm at the scan plane, under 2 returns past 4 m - and is carried for its
  orange/white diffuse map, i.e. as a camera feature.
-->
<sdf version="1.10">
  <world name="arena">

    <!-- Earth field for the ICM-20948's AK09916 magnetometer (Tesla, ENU).
         gz-sim's default value; set it for your locale if heading from the
         magnetometer needs to be meaningful. -->
    <magnetic_field>5.5645e-6 22.8758e-6 -42.3884e-6</magnetic_field>

    <physics name="1ms" type="ignored">
      <max_step_size>0.001</max_step_size>
      <real_time_factor>1.0</real_time_factor>
    </physics>

    <plugin filename="gz-sim-physics-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Physics"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-sensors-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Sensors">
      <render_engine>ogre2</render_engine>
    </plugin>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-imu-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Imu"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-magnetometer-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Magnetometer"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-scene-broadcaster-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::SceneBroadcaster"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-user-commands-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::UserCommands"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-contact-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Contact"/>

    <!-- Project-specific systems shipped by einride_mini_truck_gazebo -->
    <plugin filename="BasicSystem" name="einride_mini_truck_gazebo::BasicSystem"/>
    <plugin filename="FullSystem" name="einride_mini_truck_gazebo::FullSystem"/>

    <scene>
      <ambient>1.0 1.0 1.0</ambient>
      <background>0.85 0.87 0.90</background>
      <grid>false</grid>
    </scene>

    <!-- Three directional lights rather than one. The docks face four different
         directions, so a single sun leaves half of the bays lit only by ambient.
         Detection does not care - ambient alone gives full decision margin -
         but the scene is unreadable to a human that way. -->
    <light type="directional" name="sun">
      <cast_shadows>true</cast_shadows>
      <pose>0 0 10 0 0 0</pose>
      <diffuse>0.7 0.7 0.7 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.15 0.15 0.15 1</specular>
      <direction>-0.45 0.15 -0.88</direction>
    </light>
    <light type="directional" name="fill_x">
      <cast_shadows>false</cast_shadows>
      <pose>0 0 8 0 0 0</pose>
      <diffuse>0.35 0.35 0.37 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.05 0.05 0.05 1</specular>
      <direction>0.75 -0.15 -0.64</direction>
    </light>
    <light type="directional" name="fill_y">
      <cast_shadows>false</cast_shadows>
      <pose>0 0 8 0 0 0</pose>
      <diffuse>0.30 0.30 0.32 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.05 0.05 0.05 1</specular>
      <direction>0.05 0.80 -0.60</direction>
    </light>

    <!-- Floor: exactly the 6 x 6 m arena plus the wall footprint, top face at
         z = 0. A finite box rather than an infinite plane so the arena reads at
         its true size. -->
    <model name="arena_floor">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>0 0 -0.01 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>6.10 6.10 0.02</size></box></geometry>
          <surface>
            <friction>
              <ode><mu>1.0</mu><mu2>1.0</mu2></ode>
            </friction>
          </surface>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>6.10 6.10 0.02</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.52 0.53 0.55 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.70 0.71 0.73 1</diffuse>
            <specular>0.03 0.03 0.03 1</specular>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- Perimeter walls. Inner faces at x, y = +-3.000 exactly, so the clear
         arena is 6 x 6 m; the 50 mm thickness goes outward. 300 mm tall, which
         covers the whole mandated 80-180 mm scan band with room to spare while
         staying below the docks so they read as features against it.

         Wall length is 6.10 m so the four overlap at the corners and leave no
         gap for a stray beam to escape through. -->
    <model name="arena_wall_x_pos">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>3.025 0 0.15 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>0.05 6.10 0.30</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>0.05 6.10 0.30</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.58 0.58 0.59 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.84 0.84 0.83 1</diffuse>
            <specular>0.03 0.03 0.03 1</specular>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>
    <model name="arena_wall_x_neg">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>-3.025 0 0.15 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>0.05 6.10 0.30</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>0.05 6.10 0.30</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.58 0.58 0.59 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.84 0.84 0.83 1</diffuse>
            <specular>0.03 0.03 0.03 1</specular>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>
    <model name="arena_wall_y_pos">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>0 3.025 0.15 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>6.10 0.05 0.30</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>6.10 0.05 0.30</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.58 0.58 0.59 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.84 0.84 0.83 1</diffuse>
            <specular>0.03 0.03 0.03 1</specular>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>
    <model name="arena_wall_y_neg">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>0 -3.025 0.15 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>6.10 0.05 0.30</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>6.10 0.05 0.30</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.58 0.58 0.59 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.84 0.84 0.83 1</diffuse>
            <specular>0.03 0.03 0.03 1</specular>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- The eight docks. See the table at the top of this file. Each carries a
         different tag36h11 id and a matching pylon letter, paired in
         CMakeLists.txt so the two cannot drift apart. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://warehouse_dock_a</uri>
      <name>dock_a</name>
      <pose>2.992 -1.500 0 0 0 3.14159265</pose>
    </include>
    <include>
      <uri>model://warehouse_dock_b</uri>
      <name>dock_b</name>
      <pose>2.992 1.500 0 0 0 3.14159265</pose>
    </include>
    <include>
      <uri>model://warehouse_dock_c</uri>
      <name>dock_c</name>
      <pose>1.500 2.992 0 0 0 -1.57079633</pose>
    </include>
    <include>
      <uri>model://warehouse_dock_d</uri>
      <name>dock_d</name>
      <pose>-1.500 2.992 0 0 0 -1.57079633</pose>
    </include>
    <include>
      <uri>model://warehouse_dock_e</uri>
      <name>dock_e</name>
      <pose>-2.992 1.500 0 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>
    <include>
      <uri>model://warehouse_dock_f</uri>
      <name>dock_f</name>
      <pose>-2.992 -1.500 0 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>
    <include>
      <uri>model://warehouse_dock_g</uri>
      <name>dock_g</name>
      <pose>-1.500 -2.992 0 0 0 1.57079633</pose>
    </include>
    <include>
      <uri>model://warehouse_dock_h</uri>
      <name>dock_h</name>
      <pose>1.500 -2.992 0 0 0 1.57079633</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- ============================ props ============================
         See PROP LAYOUT in the header for why each cluster is what it is.
         All props sit on the floor at z=0: their meshes are authored with the
         base at the origin, confirmed by origin_offset_mm in each
         props/<dir>/measured.json. -->

    <!-- NE corner: three barrels in a triangle. Asymmetric about the y=x diagonal,
         which is the D4 element that would otherwise map this corner to itself. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://construction_barrel</uri>
      <name>ne_barrel_1</name>
      <pose>2.25 2.3 0 0 0 0.0</pose>
    </include>
    <include>
      <uri>model://construction_barrel</uri>
      <name>ne_barrel_2</name>
      <pose>2.65 2.3 0 0 0 0.0</pose>
    </include>
    <include>
      <uri>model://construction_barrel</uri>
      <name>ne_barrel_3</name>
      <pose>2.45 2.65 0 0 0 0.0</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- NW corner: one barrier chamfering the corner at 45 deg. A 45 deg line here is
         distinguishable from both the walls (0/90 deg) and the dock wings, which
         are 45 deg but always in a trapezoidal triple. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://jersey_barrier</uri>
      <name>nw_barrier</name>
      <pose>-2.55 2.55 0 0 0 0.785398</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- SW corner: two barrels and a cone on a diagonal line - a different count AND a
         different shape mix from NE, which is what breaks the 180 deg rotation. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://construction_barrel</uri>
      <name>sw_barrel_1</name>
      <pose>-2.7 -2.3 0 0 0 0.0</pose>
    </include>
    <include>
      <uri>model://construction_barrel</uri>
      <name>sw_barrel_2</name>
      <pose>-2.45 -2.55 0 0 0 0.0</pose>
    </include>
    <include>
      <uri>model://construction_cone</uri>
      <name>sw_cone</name>
      <pose>-2.2 -2.8 0 0 0 0.0</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- SE corner: one barrel flanked by two cones - again distinct from NW, its 180 deg
         partner, and from NE, its mirror partner. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://construction_barrel</uri>
      <name>se_barrel</name>
      <pose>2.45 -2.45 0 0 0 0.0</pose>
    </include>
    <include>
      <uri>model://construction_cone</uri>
      <name>se_cone_1</name>
      <pose>2.15 -2.75 0 0 0 0.0</pose>
    </include>
    <include>
      <uri>model://construction_cone</uri>
      <name>se_cone_2</name>
      <pose>2.75 -2.2 0 0 0 0.0</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- Interior cluster, deliberately off-centre. The two barriers overlap at the L
         corner - joined structure, not a 0 mm gap, and harmless because both are
         static so no contact is ever resolved between them. This is the occluder:
         it hides part of the arena from any single vantage point and forces the
         rover to drive around it. 588 mm clear of the spawn point at the origin. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://jersey_barrier</uri>
      <name>in_barrier_x</name>
      <pose>-0.85 1.05 0 0 0 0.0</pose>
    </include>
    <include>
      <uri>model://jersey_barrier</uri>
      <name>in_barrier_y</name>
      <pose>-1.18 0.65 0 0 0 1.570796</pose>
    </include>
    <include>
      <uri>model://construction_barrel</uri>
      <name>in_barrel</name>
      <pose>-0.55 0.4 0 0 0 0.0</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- Rover at the arena centre, facing +X - i.e. looking at docks A and B. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://einride_mini_truck</uri>
      <name>einride_mini_truck</name>
      <pose>0 0 0.01 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

  </world>
</sdf>
